A||||4||DiggityDan||Suspension and steering questions||||1||
Z||000000||DiggityDan||04-11-2000||05:46 PM||||I have a couple of questions. My first is about the steering rack bushing. Mine is loose on both sides, well changing the passenger completely fix it? How hard is it to do? Any special tools needed? Will the passenger side bushing also fit in the driver's side?<P>Next question is about the rear suspension. Will the rear end suspension from an 88 fit in my 85? I won't to fix this "bump-steer" problem and I'm just looking at all the options. Anybody done this? Anybody do something else?<BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-11-2000||07:13 PM||||no and no<P>the rack will need to be rebuilt or replaced.<P>the 88 parts will not fit the others. I don't remember who makes the bump kit. BatBoy has it. This assumes you problem is really bump steer.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||04-11-2000||07:53 PM||||Bat has the RCC bump steer kit-as to how well it works ???? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||mwbackus||04-11-2000||08:01 PM||||You can swap in an 88 rear suspension, but you will need to install the entire 88 cradle (brakes, wheel bearings, etc.) and use coil overs in order to do it.  The installation of the cradle itself is accomplished by slotting the rear mounting holes and changing the width spacing on the forward cradle mounts. Ryane Motor Sports sells the strut tower relocation adapter (part #F043 $96.00) which you will need for this swap.  (Note:  The design requires use of their coil over conversion kit.)<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/88cradle.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/88cradle.htm</A> <P>Mike B.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||04-12-2000||06:01 PM||||Yep, I have the RCC bump steer correction kit install, but I have yet to drive the Batmobile since. It's waiting for the rear sway bar, a battery, and a four wheel alignment. Been occupied building a 454, no it's not going into one of my Fieros, it's for my truck. Dan, don't worry about the bump steer thing until you have the rest of the suspension back into good working order. Fiero bump steer is really not that bad until you are really pushing it hard through the curves, like double the posted recommended yellow speed sign, and then it's only a problem if you hit a chuckhole or something. Racks do eventually wear out, but I'm guessing the biggest problem is your inner tie rods. You have to take them off to get to the rack anywa, so you might as well get new ones anyway. Slap the new inner tie rods into the rack and then see if you have the same amount of play. If you still have looseness on both sides, then it's time for a rebuilt rack.||||reg||1
